title: Genesis 6 – Sons of God
description: A study in Genesis 6:1-2: who the sons of God were, both views laid out from the King James Bible, and why it matters that God judged man.
output: lessons/genesis/genesis-6-sons-of-god.html

=== Introduction
--- Sons of God [title]
overline: A study in Genesis 6
sub: Who took the daughters of men to wife, and why it matters that God judged *man*.
refs: Genesis 6:1-2
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

--- A disagreement among brethren
lead: Who the sons of God were in Genesis 6 is a tertiary doctrine. It does not touch salvation, and faithful believers who love the King James Bible hold both of these views:
panel: Sons are believers | The sons of God were men of Seth’s believing line who married unbelieving women of the world.
panel: Sons are angels | The sons of God were fallen angels who took human women as wives.
p: We will show our work from the King James Bible alone, state each view as its holders would, and let the text decide. [[Romans 14:5]] [[Ephesians 4:3]]
point: Two lines | Cain’s family and Seth’s family, from Genesis 4 and 5
point: The case for Sons are believers |
point: The case for Sons are angels | Stated fairly, then compared text by text
point: After the Flood | Giants, possession, and where Scripture puts the blame
point: The deciding question | Would God call fallen angels His sons?

=== Two lines
--- The line of Cain
big: Genesis 4:16
- Cain builds a city and names it after his son Enoch. [[Genesis 4:16-17|Gen 4:17]]
- Lamech takes two wives, the first man recorded to do so. [[Genesis 4:19]]
- Lamech boasts to his wives that he has slain a man, and claims a vengeance greater than Cain’s. [[Genesis 4:23-24|Gen 4:23–24]]
p: A line that walks away from the presence of the {Lord} and grows in cities, crafts, and violence. Not once in Genesis 4 does anyone in this line call upon God.

--- The line of Seth
big: Genesis 4:26
p: God appoints “another seed instead of Abel,” and from that seed men begin to call on the {Lord}. Genesis 5 then traces that line by name, ten generations from Adam to Noah. [[Genesis 4:25-26|Gen 4:25–26]]
tile: 1 | Adam
tile: 2 | Seth
tile: 3 | Enos
tile: 4 | Cainan
tile: 5 | Mahalaleel
tile: 6 | Jared
tile: 7 | Enoch
tile: 8 | Methuselah
tile: 9 | Lamech
tile: 10 | Noah
p: Two families now live side by side on the earth: one that went out from the {Lord}’s presence, and one that calls on His name.

--- Two Enochs, two Lamechs
p: Moses gives both lines a man named Enoch and a man named Lamech, as if to set them side by side so we cannot miss the contrast.
parallel: Cain’s line | Seth’s line
row: **Enoch.** A city is named for him. [[Genesis 4:17]] | **Enoch.** “Enoch walked with God: and he was not; for God took him.” [[Genesis 5:24]]
row: **Lamech.** Takes two wives and boasts of slaying a man. [[Genesis 4:23-24]] | **Lamech.** Names his son Noah, hoping for comfort from the ground the {Lord} cursed. [[Genesis 5:29]]
p: Same names, opposite hearts. When Genesis 6 speaks of “sons of God” and “daughters of men,” these two families are the ones the reader has just met.

--- Noah, the last son before the Flood
big: Genesis 6:8-9
p: Ten generations after Adam, the line that called on the {Lord} comes down to one man and his house. The sons of God had married into the world around them; Noah walked with God.
callout: “Perfect in his generations” means that among the men of his own time, Noah was a believer, mature in his faith. God calls Job “perfect” in the same way, and asks it of Abram. The verse explains itself: “Noah walked with God.” [[Job 1:1]] [[Genesis 17:1]]

=== Sons are believers
--- How anyone becomes a son of God
big: John 1:12
scripture: Romans 8:14
scripture: Galatians 3:26
center: Scripture never makes sonship a matter of nature, rank, or power. It is received by faith in the Son.

--- Sons before the cross
lead: Christ is “the Lamb slain from the foundation of the world.” His work stands outside of time, and Old Testament saints believed God’s promise of Him.
panel: They looked to Christ | The Lamb slain from the foundation of the world. [[Revelation 13:8]] Abraham rejoiced to see Christ’s day. [[John 8:56]] Moses esteemed the reproach of Christ. [[Hebrews 11:26]] Seth’s line called on the name of the {Lord}. [[Genesis 4:25-26|Gen 4:26]]
panel: God called them His own | “Ye are the children of the {Lord} your God.” [[Deuteronomy 14:1]] “Bring my sons from far, and my daughters.” [[Isaiah 43:6]] “Ye are the sons of the living God.” [[Hosea 1:10]]
p: The Old Testament already uses the language of sons for God’s believing people. Genesis 6 does not need a new definition.

--- Read Genesis 6:1–7 slowly
heading: Read it slowly
scripture: Genesis 6:1-7
stat: 10 | times the passage says man or men
stat: 0 | times it says angel
p: God’s Spirit strives with man, “for that he also is flesh.” The flesh in view is man’s. The wickedness God sees is man’s. The creature God repents of making is man.

--- The order of events
point: The giants are already there | “There were giants in the earth in those days.” They are present before the text describes any union. [[Genesis 6:4]]
point: The sons of God take wives | They choose by sight, “of all which they chose,” not by faith. [[Genesis 6:1-2|Gen 6:2]]
point: Their children become famous men | “Mighty **men** which were of old, **men** of renown.” Famous **men**. The text never calls the children giants, angels, or anything but men. [[Genesis 6:4]]
point: God sees the wickedness of man | Every imagination of man’s heart was only evil continually. [[Genesis 6:5]]
point: God repents that He made man, and will destroy man | [[Genesis 6:6-7|Gen 6:6–7]]
point: But Noah found grace | [[Genesis 6:8-9|Gen 6:8]]
note: The judgment falls on the godly line’s compromise and the world’s wickedness alike. Nowhere does God name a hybrid race as the cause.

--- Job 1 and 2
heading: Job 1 and 2: the sons of God in heaven
big: Job 1:6
panel: Who stands before God | The heavenly assembly includes “the spirits of just men made perfect.” Redeemed men stand in God’s presence. [[Hebrews 12:22-23|Heb 12:22–23]]
panel: Among them, not of them | Satan comes “among them” as the accuser, there to charge Job. He is never counted as one of the sons. [[Job 1:9-11|Job 1:9–11]] [[Job 2:1]]
p: Repeating the scene in chapter 2 adds nothing new: the same sons, and the same accuser in their midst. [[Job 2:1]]

--- Job 38:7, who is the corner stone?
heading: Job 38:7: who is the corner stone?
center: The strongest text for Sons are angels, and the answer rests on one question.
scripture: Job 38:4-7 | mark: the foundations of the earth; the corner stone
callout-honest: **Who are the morning stars?** Angels. Scripture pictures angels as stars: Lucifer is the “son of the morning,” and the dragon’s tail drew “the third part of the stars of heaven.” The plural morning stars are not to be confused with Christ, who alone is “the bright and morning star.” [[Isaiah 14:12]] [[Revelation 12:4]] [[Revelation 22:16]]
p: So verse 7 names two companies, angels singing and the sons of God shouting. And God names two works: the foundations of the earth, and the corner stone. The sons of God shout when the corner stone is laid. So when was Christ laid as the corner stone?
point: Before the foundation of the world | The Lamb was foreordained before the foundation of the world, and is the Lamb slain from its foundation. [[1 Peter 1:19-20|1 Pet 1:19–20]] [[Revelation 13:8]]
point: At creation, He is the Creator | “In the beginning was the Word… and the Word was God. All things were made by him.” At creation Christ is the Builder, not a stone laid in His own building. If the corner stone were laid at creation, the Creator would become part of the creation. [[John 1:1-3|John 1:1–3]] [[Colossians 1:16]]
point: In time, God lays the corner stone | God promised to lay it “in Zion” ([[Isaiah 28:16]]). It was laid when He came in the flesh, was crucified and set at nought by the builders, and was raised from the dead. At the resurrection God fulfilled “Thou art my Son, this day have I begotten thee.” [[Acts 4:10-11|Acts 4:10–11]] [[Acts 13:33]] [[Ephesians 2:20]]
callout: **The question:** is laying the corner stone the same event as founding the earth? Sons are angels reads verses 4–7 as one creation scene, and on a first reading that is natural. Sons are believers sees two works, linked but separate: the earth founded by Christ the Creator, and Christ Himself laid as the corner stone in His triumph over death. At that, the morning stars (the angels) sing, and the sons of God, the redeemed, shout for joy, as Abraham rejoiced to see His day. [[John 8:56]]

--- Angels are not sons, and do not marry
scripture: Hebrews 1:5
scripture: Hebrews 1:14
scripture: Matthew 22:30
scripture: Mark 12:25
refs: Hebrews 1:13-14
center: Angels serve the heirs of salvation; they are not the heirs. And the Lord Jesus uses angels as the very picture of beings who do not marry.

=== Sons are angels
--- Sons are angels, stated fairly
lead: Many faithful believers hold that the sons of God were fallen angels who took human wives. Most reject the Book of Enoch and build the case from the King James Bible. Stated as its holders would state it:
- In Job, the sons of God stand before the {Lord} in heaven, so Genesis 6 should be read the same way. [[Job 1:6]] [[Job 2:1]]
- Job 38:7 places the sons of God at the founding of the earth, before man was made. [[Job 38:4-7|Job 38:4–7]]
- Setting “sons of God” against “daughters of men” marks two different kinds of being. [[Genesis 6:1-2|Gen 6:2]]
- Angels have taken bodies, eaten, and been taken for men. [[Genesis 18:8]] [[Genesis 19:1]] [[Hebrews 13:2]]
- Matthew 22:30 describes holy angels in heaven, not the ones who “left their own habitation.” [[Matthew 22:30]] [[Jude 6]]
- Jude, 2 Peter, and 1 Peter tie angelic sin to “strange flesh” and to the days of Noah. [[Jude 1:4-8|Jude 4–8]] [[2 Peter 2:4-9|2 Pet 2:4–9]] [[1 Peter 3:18-20|1 Pet 3:18–20]]
- The children became “mighty men… of renown,” something beyond the ordinary. [[Genesis 6:4]]
callout-honest: We grant that this is a real case for angels, built from Scripture by people who love the Book. Notice, though, which angels it needs: not holy ones, but angels who had fallen or were falling in that very act. We will come back to that.

--- The proof texts, side by side
parallel: Sons are angels | Sons are believers
row: [[Job 38:4-7]] *(strongest text)* The sons of God shout for joy when the earth’s foundations are laid, before Adam existed, so they must be angels. | God names two works: the earth’s foundations, and the corner stone. At creation Christ is the Creator ([[John 1:1-3|John 1:1–3]]), not a stone laid in His own building. The corner stone is laid in time: Christ made head of the corner when God raised Him from the dead ([[Acts 4:10-11|Acts 4:10–11]], [[Ephesians 2:20]]). The sons rejoice in His triumph; the morning stars are angels, named apart from them ([[Isaiah 14:12]], [[Revelation 12:4]]).
row: [[Job 1:6]], [[Job 2:1]] An assembly before God in heaven is an assembly of angels. | Redeemed men stand in heaven ([[Hebrews 12:23]]). Satan comes “among them” as accuser, not as a son.
row: [[Genesis 6:2]] “Sons of God” against “daughters of men” marks two kinds of being. | It marks two kinds of people, as Scripture sets “the children of this world” against “the children of light” ([[Luke 16:8]]). Both are men.
row: [[Genesis 6:4]] The unions produced the giants, or at least the extraordinary mighty men. | The giants were already “in the earth in those days.” The children became “men of renown,” famous men, never called giants or hybrids.
row: [[Genesis 18:8]], [[Genesis 19:1]], [[Hebrews 13:2]] *(strongest text)* Angels can take bodies, eat, and be handled as men. | An angel is a messenger, appearing as a man or as a winged creature as God sends him ([[Isaiah 6:2]]). Angels are ministering spirits for the heirs of salvation ([[Hebrews 1:14]]), never physically mankind. None weds or begets.
row: [[Matthew 22:30]], [[Mark 12:25]] These speak of holy angels “in heaven,” not angels who left their estate. | Jesus states what angels are, not only where they are. Leaving a habitation does not grant a new nature.
row: [[Jude 1:6-7]] “In like manner… going after strange flesh” ties the angels’ sin to Sodom’s. | Jude is warning about “certain men crept in unawares” (v. 4). He lists the judged: Israel destroyed in the wilderness, angels reserved in chains, Sodom and Gomorrha. Then: “Likewise also these filthy dreamers” (v. 8). It is a list of rebels destroyed, not a link between angels and strange flesh. Hell itself was prepared “for the devil and his angels” ([[Matthew 25:41]]).
row: [[2 Peter 2:4-5]] The angels that sinned are named right beside the Flood. | Peter makes the same comparison as Jude, against false teachers (v. 1): angels, the old world, Sodom, then Lot delivered. The point is that “the Lord knoweth how… to reserve the unjust unto the day of judgment” (v. 9), not that one judgment caused the next.
row: [[1 Peter 3:19-20]] The spirits in prison were disobedient in the days of Noah. | The spirits in prison are the disobedient of Noah’s day: scorners who heard Noah, “a preacher of righteousness” ([[2 Peter 2:5]]), while the ark was preparing, and were killed in the Flood.
row: [[Genesis 6:9]] “Perfect in his generations” means Noah’s line was untouched by the unions. | Among the men of his time, Noah was a believer, mature in his faith. God calls Job “perfect” too ([[Job 1:1]], [[Genesis 17:1]]). “Noah walked with God.”

--- Jude 6, honestly
big: Jude 6
refs: Jude 1:4-8
panel: What it says | Some angels did not keep their first estate. They left their own habitation. They are held in chains for the judgment.
panel: What it does not say | When they left. Anything about women, marriage, or children. Anything about the Flood.
callout: **The Sons are believers reading, held with an open hand:** unclean spirits seek a habitation in men. The wickedness of Noah’s day may well have included spirits possessing men, as they still do, with no bodies, wives, or children of their own. Where Scripture is not plain, we should not build a doctrine. [[Matthew 12:43-45|Matt 12:43–45]]

--- Angels in the form of men
callout-honest: **What Sons are angels rightly sees:** angels have appeared as men, eaten a meal, and taken men by the hand. Some have entertained angels unawares. [[Genesis 18:8]] [[Genesis 19:1]] [[Hebrews 13:2]]
p: **What Scripture also shows**
- An angel is a messenger sent from God, and appears in whatever form God sends him: as men at Abraham’s tent, as six-winged seraphims in Isaiah’s vision. [[Isaiah 6:2]]
- Hebrews calls them “ministering spirits, sent forth to minister for them who shall be heirs of salvation.” They are spirits who serve the sons of God, not mankind in the flesh. [[Hebrews 1:13-14|Heb 1:14]]
- At Sodom, the angels are the ones defended, not the ones defiled. The lust belongs to the men of the city, and the angels strike them blind. [[Genesis 19:10-11|Gen 19:10–11]]
- No angel who appears as a man ever weds a woman or fathers a child.
center: Appearing as a man for a message is not becoming a man for a marriage.

=== After the Flood
--- The Flood settled it either way
lead: Whatever those unions were, God destroyed them. “Noah only remained alive, and they that were with him in the ark.” [[Genesis 7:23]]
panel: How Jesus told it | In Noah’s days they were eating, drinking, marrying, and giving in marriage. Ordinary marriages of ordinary men. [[Matthew 24:37-39|Matt 24:37–39]] [[Luke 17:26-27|Luke 17:26–27]]
panel: Never again | After the Flood, Scripture never records another union of angels and women. If it happened once, it left no trace in the rest of the Book.
panel: Still today | Possession by unclean spirits appears again and again, and missionaries still meet it. [[Mark 5:2|Mark 5]] [[Acts 16:16]]
center: One explanation fits what Scripture keeps showing us. The other requires an event Scripture never shows again.

--- Giants are men
p: Genesis 6:4 says there were giants “also after that.” The rest of the Bible shows exactly that: tall men, after the Flood, with no angels involved.
- The sons of Anak, “which come of the giants.” [[Numbers 13:33]]
- The Emims, “a people great, and many, and tall.” [[Deuteronomy 2:10-11|Deut 2:10–11]]
- Og of Bashan, whose iron bed was nine cubits long, about thirteen and a half feet. [[Deuteronomy 3:11]]
- Goliath of Gath, six cubits and a span, nearly ten feet. [[1 Samuel 17:4]]
- A man of great stature with six fingers and six toes, “born to the giant,” slain by David’s men. [[2 Samuel 21:20-22|2 Sam 21:20–22]]
callout: In living memory, Robert Wadlow (1918–1940) stood 8 feet 11 inches tall. Great stature is a human trait, then and now. It needs no angelic father.

--- Do not shift the blame
scripture: Genesis 3:12-13
p: Since the garden, man has pointed somewhere else. God answered each one for his own sin.
scripture: James 1:14
callout: In Genesis 6, God names one guilty party: man. Even if spirits were at work in that day, His verdict is “the wickedness of man.” A teaching that moves the weight of Genesis 6 onto a demonic host moves it where God did not put it, under either view. [[Genesis 6:5]]

=== The deciding question
--- Grant that they were angels
lead: Suppose the case for angels is right. Then they were not holy angels, who keep their estate. They were fallen angels, rebels against God in the very act of Genesis 6. So the question becomes: would God call those His sons?
panel-warn: What Scripture says of fallen angels | They share the devil’s final destination: hell, “prepared for the devil and his angels.” [[Matthew 25:41]] Even wicked men are called “children of the devil” and “children of disobedience.” [[1 John 3:10]] [[Ephesians 2:2|Eph 2:2]] [[John 8:44]] God never calls an angel His son, holy or fallen. [[Hebrews 1:5]]
panel: What Scripture says of sons who sin | They are still sons. Just Lot was a righteous man while he vexed his soul in Sodom. [[2 Peter 2:7-8|2 Pet 2:7–8]] God’s sons are warned to come out and be separate, not yoked with unbelievers. [[2 Corinthians 6:14|2 Cor 6:14]] [[2 Corinthians 6:17-18|2 Cor 6:17–18]] Israel was not to marry the nations, “for they will turn away thy son from following me.” [[Deuteronomy 7:3-4|Deut 7:3–4]]

--- Sons are made in His image
big: Genesis 1:27
refs: Genesis 1:26-27
panel: Man | Made in the image and likeness of God. Christ took on Him the seed of Abraham, so that men might become sons by faith. [[Hebrews 2:16]] [[John 1:12]]
panel: Angels | Spirits, created by Him and for His purpose, not made in His likeness. [[Colossians 1:16]] [[Hebrews 1:14]] Salvation is never offered to them: “he took not on him the nature of angels.” [[Hebrews 2:16]]
callout: James, answering what “a man may say,” notes that “the devils also believe, and tremble.” Even so, that is not belief unto salvation, because saving grace was never extended to them. Sonship by faith is offered only to the creature made in God’s image. [[James 2:18-19|Jas 2:18–19]]

--- A note on the fringe
lead: The Book of Enoch, the “Watchers,” and giants thousands of feet tall are not found in the Bible, and they have no place in this study. Most who hold Sons are angels reject them as well.
p: Jude’s quoting one prophecy of Enoch no more makes that book Scripture than Paul’s quoting pagan poets makes their writings Scripture.
refs: Jude 14; Acts 17:28; Titus 1:12; Proverbs 30:6

=== Close
--- Be fully persuaded [title]
overline: Romans 14:5
heading: Be fully persuaded
big: Romans 14:5
p: We have laid out both views and shown our work. Now search the Scriptures for yourself, as the Bereans did, and weigh these questions: [[Acts 17:11]]
point: Who does Scripture call the sons of God? |
point: Who is the corner stone of Job 38? |
point: Whose wickedness did God judge in Genesis 6? |
point: Would God call fallen angels His sons? |
hint: Whichever way you land, Noah found grace in the eyes of the {Lord}, by faith. Hold your view with conviction, and your brethren with charity.
